    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n## Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nProduct roadmaps often fail because they promise certainty where none exists. They turn assumptions about customers, markets, and technology into schedules that look authoritative. A feature appears beside a quarter, stakeholders make plans around it, and an educated guess quietly becomes a commitment. When reality changes, the roadmap becomes either irrelevant or an obstacle to good judgment.\n\nThe first problem is confusing outputs with outcomes. Many roadmaps list what a team will build without explaining what should improve. “Launch a dashboard” is concrete, but it does not establish whether customers need better visibility, faster decisions, or fewer reporting tasks. A team can deliver every planned feature and still fail to create meaningful value. Shipping is evidence of activity, not necessarily progress.\n\nA second problem is that roadmaps frequently encode organizational politics rather than product strategy. Sales wants features that might close deals. Executives want visible innovation. Existing customers want their requests prioritized. Without a clear strategic objective, the roadmap becomes a negotiated collection of demands. Each item may be defensible on its own, but together they produce a scattered product and an overloaded team.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they treat learning as disruption. Product development involves discovering which problems matter and which solutions work. Research, experiments, and early releases should change what happens next. Yet teams are often judged on adherence to the original plan. This creates a perverse incentive: ignore new evidence, preserve the schedule, and deliver something nobody needs. The roadmap stops guiding decisions and starts protecting past decisions from scrutiny.\n\nAnother weakness is false precision. Long-range plans rarely account adequately for technical dependencies, maintenance, staffing changes, or the complexity hidden inside apparently simple requests. Detailed dates conceal this uncertainty rather than resolve it. Once those dates circulate, revising them can damage trust—even when revision is the most responsible choice.\n\nThe answer is not to abandon roadmaps. Teams still need direction, coordination, and a way to communicate priorities. But a useful roadmap should distinguish firm commitments from tentative possibilities. It should connect proposed work to measurable outcomes, make assumptions explicit, and provide room for discovery. Near-term work can be specific; longer-term work should emphasize problems and strategic intent rather than fixed delivery dates.\n\nUltimately, a roadmap succeeds when it helps an organization make better choices, including the choice not to build something. Its value lies less in predicting the future than in creating a shared basis for responding to it. Roadmaps fail when they are treated as contracts with reality. They become useful when they are treated as plans that evidence is allowed to change.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nProduct roadmaps are meant to align teams, set expectations, and guide execution toward valuable outcomes. Yet most fail—not because planning is inherently flawed, but because they are treated as rigid contracts rather than living hypotheses. The result is missed deadlines, wasted effort, eroded trust, and products that miss the market.\n\nA core reason is the absence of a clear strategy. Many roadmaps are feature lists disconnected from business goals or customer problems. Teams commit to shipping “the dashboard,” “the mobile app,” or “AI features” without defining success metrics or the underlying jobs-to-be-done. When priorities shift or data contradicts assumptions, the plan has no north star, so work continues on items that no longer matter.\n\nOvercommitment compounds the problem. Stakeholders demand dates and scope; product managers, seeking alignment, pack the timeline with more than capacity allows. Engineering realities—technical debt, unknowns, dependencies—are underestimated. The roadmap becomes a promise rather than a forecast, guaranteeing disappointment when delivery slips.\n\nPoor communication and lack of shared ownership accelerate failure. A roadmap created in isolation by product or leadership is rarely understood or believed by engineering, design, sales, or customers. Without regular, transparent updates and genuine input, it is ignored or gamed. Cross-functional dependencies surface too late, and political pressure to “hit the date” overrides learning.\n\nFinally, most roadmaps ignore change. Markets, competitors, user behavior, and technology evolve. Treating the document as static—rather than a set of bets that must be validated, killed, or adjusted—guarantees irrelevance. Teams that refuse to re-prioritize or cut scope when evidence arrives waste quarters on the original plan.\n\nSuccessful roadmaps invert these patterns: they start from outcomes, remain visibly tentative, involve the people who will execute them, and are revisited frequently with data. The ones that fail do the opposite. They confuse activity with progress and commitment with certainty. In a world of uncertainty, that is a recipe for waste.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
